<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiridates III of Armenia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 330</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Tiridates the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tiridates III was Arsacid king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> from the late 3rd century until about 330, restored with Roman support after a period of Sasanian rule. Converted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Gregory the Illuminator</a>, he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the state religion of Armenia in about 301, traditionally the first kingdom to do so, and backed the founding of the cathedral of Etchmiadzin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiridates-iii-of-armenia.jpg" alt="Tiridates III of Armenia" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gaidzakian, Ohan, 1837-1914 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiridates_III_illustration.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tiridates III was the son of Khosrov II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, who was assassinated in 252 by a Parthian agent named Anak acting under orders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a>. Anak and most of his family were executed, while his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Gregory the Illuminator</a> was sheltered in Caesaria, in Cappadocia. As the only surviving heir, Tiridates was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as an infant. He was educated there, skilled in languages and military tactics, and understood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>. His sister was Khosrovidukht. The name Tiridates means created by Tir, a Zoroastrian divinity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the Roman emperor Aurelian pushed the Sassanids back in 270, Tiridates came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> as heir to the Persian-occupied throne, raised an army and drove the enemy out in 298. He then carried his arms into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, but when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> won a civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, Tiridates took refuge with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> again. With the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> he drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> out of Armenia, and in 299 Diocletian left Armenia in a quasi-independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, possibly as a buffer. In 297 Tiridates married an Alanian princess, Ashkhen; their children included Khosrov III and Salome.</p><p>The traditional story of his conversion rests mainly on the fifth-century history attributed to Agathangelos. Gregory, the son of Anak, was brought up a Christian and served the king as secretary. When he refused to place a wreath before the statue of the goddess Anahit, and was revealed as Anak's son, Tiridates had him thrown into the dungeon of Khor Virap. Later, after the king had tortured and killed a group of nuns led by Gayane who had fled Roman persecution, he fell ill. Gregory, found alive after 13 years, cured him, and Tiridates proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the state religion and appointed Gregory Catholicos. Mary Boyce considers that the Christianisation was partly in defiance of the Sassanids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> was deeply rooted, and Tiridates often used force to impose the new faith. Armed conflicts followed, including a battle between his forces and the Zoroastrian camp, and he spent the rest of his life trying to eliminate the old beliefs, destroying many statues, temples and written documents; as a result little is known from local sources about ancient Armenian culture. He died in 330, and Movses Khorenatsi says that nakharar families conspired against him and poisoned him. Tiridates, Ashkhen and Khosrovidukht are saints of the Armenian Apostolic Church, with a feast day around 30 June, and Tiridates is also venerated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiridates III of Armenia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Tiridates III</b> adopted Christianity around 301 through Gregory the Illuminator, and Etchmiadzin cathedral was founded.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
